---
import StoryLayout from "../../layouts/StoryLayout.astro";
import StoryChapter from "../../components/StoryChapter.astro";
import CaseJourney from "../../components/CaseJourney.astro";
import ArchitectureCompare from "../../components/ArchitectureCompare.astro";

const steps = [
  {
    key: "site",
    label: "Moving to Fargate",
    text: [
      "The site moved from EC2-hosted containers to AWS ECS Fargate and RDS. From the migration until I left in February 2026, it had no further downtime.",
    ],
    caption: "jisc.ac.uk today: the Solutions menu, then the latest news. The hosting is invisible from here, which is the point.",
  },
  {
    key: "carousel",
    label: "The carousel",
    text: [
      "I built the site's carousel, end to end: the one on the home page that pages through the latest news, blogs and projects.",
    ],
    caption: "The “Latest from Jisc” carousel on the home page, stepped through with its Next control.",
  },
  {
    key: "publishing",
    label: "Publishing",
    text: [
      "Editors work in Wagtail and Next.js presents the site. I built Wagtail components and kept both sides working through the move, so an editorial change still appeared the way it should.",
    ],
    caption: "From the content hub into a news article.",
  },
];

const chapters = [
  { id: "problem", label: "The problem" },
  { id: "how-it-works", label: "How it works" },
  { id: "what-happened", label: "What happened" },
  {
    id: "under-the-hood",
    label: "Under the hood",
    children: [
      { id: "fargate", label: "Fargate and previews" },
      { id: "registry", label: "A private npm registry" },
      { id: "components", label: "Components that never broke a page" },
      { id: "architecture", label: "Architecture" },
    ],
  },
  { id: "lessons", label: "What I learned" },
];
---

<StoryLayout
  title="The Jisc website"
  description="Moving jisc.ac.uk from failing EC2 containers to AWS ECS Fargate, and retiring Wagtail components without breaking a page, in time for Digifest."
  chapters={chapters}
  groups={[{ id: "website", threads: steps }]}
>
  <StoryChapter id="problem" title="The problem">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Researchers check publishing policies on <a href="/work/open-policy-finder">Open Policy Finder</a>. Everyone else meets Jisc through <a href="https://www.jisc.ac.uk">jisc.ac.uk</a>, which content teams keep up to date.</p>
      <p>The daily outages came from EC2-hosted containers that ran out of memory, on top of a DDoS and a misconfigured network.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="how-it-works" title="How it works" group="website">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>I stepped into the Digital Experience Team's infrastructure work under a tight deadline, while still maintaining the publishing system. Three parts, recorded on the live site.</p>
    </div>
    <CaseJourney project="jisc-website" steps={steps} />
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="what-happened" title="What happened">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>The main website had no more downtime from the migration until I left in February 2026.</p>
      <p>Old components were retired and pages moved onto new ones without a page breaking, in time for company deadlines like Digifest, Jisc's festival.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="under-the-hood" title="Under the hood" part>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>For engineers: the hosting and release path, a fix for shared builds, and how the components changed under live pages.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="fargate" title="Fargate and previews" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>The move to Fargate stopped the outages. From the migration until I left in February 2026, the site had no more downtime.</p>
      <p>Before it, Wagtail and Next.js ran in containers on EC2 servers that kept running out of memory. Long migrations competed with the live site for the same resources. The network had the wrong VPC, gateway and firewall settings, and DDoS-like traffic piled on top.</p>
      <p>The site went down at least once a day. Someone reset it by hand in the AWS console, or the budget went up so it could limp on. By my estimate, uptime was around 60–70%.</p>
      <p>I owned the cost appraisal: the options, what each would cost and what it would fix. We moved both halves of the site, Wagtail for content and Next.js for the public pages, into containers that AWS runs and scales for us. There is no server of ours left to fill up. That service is ECS Fargate.</p>
      <p>The database moved to RDS, AWS's managed database. A status page polls health checks on the front end and the API, and a failure pages whoever is on call.</p>
      <p>The release path changed too. Changes used to be checked on a staging site that few people used. Now each pull request gets its own temporary copy of the site with only that change in it, and QA and reviewers try the change there before it merges.</p>
      <p>Engineers call these ephemeral preview environments. Terraform, which builds cloud resources from files in the repository, sets each one up the same way.</p>
      <p>Playwright tests, which click through the site in a real browser, ran as part of each release. Testing got easier, and the staging site was retired, along with what it cost to run.</p>
    </div>
    <ArchitectureCompare
      id="arch-jisc"
      before={{
        title: "The Jisc website before the move",
        summary:
          "Before: Wagtail and Next.js ran in containers on EC2, next to a staging site few people used and leftover environments that were never torn down because Terraform's record of them had drifted. The containers ran out of memory, long migrations competed for resources, and the VPC, gateway and firewall settings were wrong. jisc.ac.uk went down at least daily.",
      }}
      after={{
        title: "The Jisc website after the move",
        summary:
          "After: Wagtail manages content and Next.js presents it, both on AWS ECS Fargate, with the database on RDS. Each pull request gets its own Terraform-managed preview in place of the staging site, and Playwright checks run as part of release. A status page polls health checks. jisc.ac.uk had no downtime after the move.",
      }}
      width={720}
      height={396}
      groups={[
        { id: "ec2", label: "EC2 containers", in: "before", x: 20, y: 20, w: 290, h: 356 },
        { id: "fargate", label: "AWS ECS Fargate", in: "after", x: 20, y: 20, w: 450, h: 104 },
      ]}
      boxes={[
        { id: "wagtail", label: "Wagtail", note: "editors manage content", x: 40, y: 52, w: 200 },
        { id: "next", label: "Next.js", note: "public pages", x: 260, y: 52, w: 200, from: { x: 40, y: 116 } },
        { id: "site", label: "jisc.ac.uk", weak: "down at least daily", fix: "no downtime after the move", x: 500, y: 52, w: 200, from: { x: 500, y: 116 } },
        { id: "staging", label: "Staging site", weak: "few people used it", in: "before", x: 40, y: 180, w: 250 },
        { id: "leftover", label: "Leftover environments", weak: "not torn down: state drifted", in: "before", x: 40, y: 244, w: 250 },
        { id: "rds", label: "RDS", note: "database", in: "after", x: 40, y: 164, w: 200 },
        { id: "status", label: "Status page", note: "polls health checks", in: "after", x: 500, y: 164, w: 200 },
        { id: "preview", label: "Preview per PR", note: "Terraform-managed", fix: "replaced staging", in: "after", x: 40, y: 272, w: 200, h: 70 },
        { id: "playwright", label: "Playwright", note: "release checks", in: "after", x: 280, y: 272, w: 190, h: 70 },
      ]}
      notes={[
        { text: "ran out of memory", tone: "weak", x: 34, y: 322 },
        { text: "migrations competed for resources", tone: "weak", x: 34, y: 342 },
        { text: "VPC, gateway, firewall misconfigured", tone: "weak", x: 34, y: 362 },
      ]}
      edges={[
        { from: "wagtail", to: "next" },
        { from: "next", to: "site" },
        { from: "rds", to: "fargate", plain: true },
        { from: "status", to: "site", label: "health checks" },
        { from: "preview", to: "playwright" },
        { from: "playwright", to: "fargate", label: "release" },
      ]}
    />
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="registry" title="A private npm registry" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>One smaller thing I'm fond of: I took a shared licence limit out of several teams' builds.</p>
      <p>Jisc's websites use Font Awesome for icons, and the licensed usage was shared across Jisc teams. Every website build downloaded the icon packages from Font Awesome again. When the shared usage ran out early, deployments were blocked.</p>
      <p>So I set up a cache of the packages our website builds used, and builds installed from it instead. That cache is a private npm registry: our own store of JavaScript packages, which builds fetch from in place of the public one.</p>
      <p>I rolled it out to several teams. Their builds stopped downloading from Font Awesome, so the shared limit was no longer something a deployment could run into.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="components" title="Components that never broke a page" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Pages on jisc.ac.uk are built from components in Wagtail, the CMS the content team edits in, and building those components was part of my job. Some old ones had to be replaced, often against a deadline like Digifest, Jisc's yearly festival. The catch: delete a component that live pages still use, and those pages break.</p>
      <p>So each replacement went in three steps. First I shipped the new component next to the old one, so both worked. Then the content team moved pages across, doing the editorial side while I wrote the migrations that moved the stored content. Only when no page used the old component did I delete it.</p>
      <p>Engineers call this expand and contract: grow to support both, then shrink back to one. No page broke along the way, and each change landed in time, Digifest included.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="lessons" title="What I learned">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>A public website's reliability, its editorial workflow and its release path have to work together. Fixing the hosting meant understanding how people changed and checked the site.</p>
      <p>And on a site people publish to every day, a breaking change is a schedule, not a moment: add the new thing, move everything across, then remove the old one.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>
</StoryLayout>
